const PART: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "tt:0": [
    "In Tobago, Kaila walked with her grandmother on a path through the trees. She wore a carnival costume with feathers and a flower in her hair. They were going out to catch crabs, and Grandmother carried a pot. She had asked only for what would fill the bottom of it. That was for a rice dish for everyone back home. Among the leaves a calm gentleman appeared, with a soft beard and goat's hooves. It looked like a neighbour from the woods and did not run at them. Grandmother tilted her head, without fear and without stepping back. 'He is the father of the forest, called Papa Bois, and he protects the animals,' she said.",
    "He spoke quietly, like someone asking permission, and not like someone making a threat. He did not change anyone's shape either. 'Take only what the pot needs, because the little ones are still growing,' he said, leaning on a wide tree. He promised no punishment and he did not make his voice deep. He simply waited, still, with his hooves firm on the ground. Kaila felt the feathers move in the wind, and she waved, serious, her flower held fast. Grandmother said thank you, and on they went with the measure already agreed. Nobody looked back in fear.",
    "In the good mud Kaila saw many crabs, and her hand filled fast. She took more than Grandmother had asked for the pot. The little ones fitted in her palm and waved their claws without pinching her skin. They were light as dry leaves, and she meant to drop them in the bucket with the big ones. They were pretty and easy to catch. Then she remembered the calm voice and stopped, the bucket still light in her other hand. 'These are still small,' she said, and she put them back in the mud, one by one.",
    "She kept only the ones the pot had asked for. When she looked back, the father of the forest was no longer on the path. Perhaps he was so still that he looked like a trunk among the others. Nobody had been chased, and the wood had not closed the way out or the light. Grandmother praised the hand that knew how to open. They carried the light bucket home without adding another word. The small crabs stayed in the mud, alive, and Kaila did not go back to fetch even one more. She took a loose feather from her face and helped count the crabs for the rice.",
    "At the table Kaila told the story without turning it into a fright. Grandmother said the father of the forest is pleased when the pot is not greedy. The carnival feathers dried on a hanger, far from the stove, still with a grain of sand at the tip. Kaila felt bigger for giving the small ones back. The rice reached the whole table. If you go out to gather food, take only what the pot needs. You can give the little ones back and leave the wood in peace.",
  ],
  "tt:1": [
    "On a trail in Tobago, Kaila walked with her mum. She wore a carnival costume with feathers and a flower in her hair. She counted her steps so as not to fall behind. Among the ferns they heard a thin little voice. It was calling her name, asking her to come and play off the path. Her mum gripped her hand at once and did not let go, even when the calling sounded sweet. 'They are the douen, little beings with backward feet, who call children out to play,' she said. 'The rule is simple, stay with me,' she added.",
    "And Kaila did not let go of her fingers. The voice called again, sweetly, and Kaila felt curiosity pull at her name. It was like a thin thread between the leaves. She took one step, only one, and her mum did not pull hard. She waited for Kaila to choose with her own mouth. Kaila stopped, and the feathers brushed her cheek. It was a soft warning that she was not alone. 'I am not going, I am with my mum,' she said. She said it loud enough for the ferns to hear the whole sentence. There were no hands coming out of the ground, and no running.",
    "There was only a calling and a clear answer. Her mum crouched down and showed her some small marks from a distance. They were turned the wrong way and did not lead to their own feet. 'The feet point backwards, so the trail confuses people,' she explained. 'That is why we do not follow the voice or the marks.' Kaila looked, and then looked at her own shoes, straight on the path. She held her mum's hand tighter, and the thin voice fell silent. It was like someone realising there would be no game that day. The flower in her hair stayed where it was, and nobody had left the marked way.",
    "They went on along the wide path, singing a kitchen song quietly. They wanted the forest to hear that they were together. Kaila did not answer when she thought she heard her name again. It was further off, among leaves that barely moved. Her mum praised her stopping and the loudness of the sentence said in time. Nobody hurried to reach home. The feathers flapped and made a soft sound, almost saying she was there, but not alone. They reached the sunny road, and the trail stayed behind, with nobody carried off by the wrong voice.",
    "At home, Kaila told her grandfather that she had heard her name. She had given her hand instead of taking the step towards the ferns. He nodded, proud, and did not ask for frightened details or a second trip up the trail that day. The costume sat on a chair, still cheerful, with the flower slowly wilting in a glass of water. If you hear your name coming from among the trees, stop and take an adult's hand. You do not need to go and play with someone you cannot see.",
  ],
  "tt:2": [
    "In a garden in Tobago, a hummingbird hovered in front of a long flower. Its wings made a hum like a pulled string. Kaila wore her carnival costume with feathers and a flower in her hair. For the first time that hot afternoon she was standing very still. Grandmother whispered so that a loud sentence would not scare the bird away. 'He drinks and he helps the flower without breaking it,' she said. 'And his wings are not a toy to be shut in a hand.' Kaila nodded, but her fingers were already curling in the air. They were looking for one second of touch.",
    "The hummingbird came so close that she saw its breast shine, small and whole, with no need of a cage. The wish to have a moment made her hand rise in a cup above the flower. Grandmother did not shout and did not strike her wrist. She simply stayed beside her, watching. Kaila remembered the sentence and opened her fingers before they touched the feathers. Her hand stayed open like a leaf. The hummingbird moved to another flower, still trusting, and the costume feathers stayed quiet.",
    "They watched his work, his beak going into the flower and out again. The flower was still whole, upright, with no petal on the ground because of the visit. Kaila compared that with her wish to close her hand. She felt relief that she had opened it in time. She did not fetch a jar. She did not cover the flower with her palm to trap him inside for a minute. Grandmother smiled and offered her the bench so her arms could rest far from the flight. Kaila sat down and left her hands in sight, open, as gentle proof.",
    "When the hummingbird went to the back of the garden, Kaila did not creep after it. She did not tire it out or make it lose its way. She watered the flower with a glass, slowly, so it would have something to give at the next morning's visit. A loose feather fell from her costume on to the earth. She picked it up without mistaking it for a bird's. 'This one is mine, and his stays on him,' she said, showing the feather to Grandmother on the bench. Grandmother gave her a juice, and they sat in the shade.",
    "They listened to the garden without shutting it in their hands. At the end of the afternoon the hum came back once. Kaila showed her open hands, laughing, without jumping up from the bench. The hummingbird did his work and left, and the flower was still on its stem, ready for early tomorrow. Kaila understood that helping can mean not closing. And a garden does not need to be grabbed to be loved. If you see a hummingbird on a flower, open your fingers and keep still. You do not need to hold it for the garden to be yours too.",
  ],
  "tt:3": [
    "In Port of Spain, Kaila's cousin showed her a big tin, empty and clean, in the yard at home. Kaila wore her carnival costume with feathers and a flower in her hair. She was ready for a party that had not yet begun. Her cousin ran his hand over the tin with quiet pride, like someone introducing a proper instrument. 'Our music was born when someone wanted rhythm and used metal,' he said. 'A steel drum can sing. It is not for breaking things and not for disturbing the neighbour,' he added. Kaila touched the tin with one finger.",
    "The cousin tapped with his fingertips, a round sound, then a sharper one on the rim. Then he stopped, so that the silence could be part of it too. There was no anger in the story he told. There were only people who wanted music and patient hands, over time. Kaila wanted to hit it hard, to make the feathers jump. She raised her hand quickly above the tin. Her cousin shook his head, smiling, and put the tin between them. He did not put it against the yard wall. 'First the rhythm,' he said, 'then we see if the neighbour is still resting.'",
    "He guided her fingers in a short beat on the empty tin. The sound came out beautiful and low, like a conversation without hurry. Kaila repeated it three times without speeding up. She did not drag the tin along the ground to make it speak louder. The neighbour on the other side of the wall did not shut her window. She even waved, because the yard was pleasant. Kaila felt a warm pride in her chest, different from just making a noise because the tin existed. The flower in her hair followed the movement of her head, light, in the same beat.",
    "When Kaila wanted to carry on much louder, her cousin covered the tin with his palm. He did not hurt her hand, and he did not take her turn away for ever. 'We stop before we disturb,' he said. They put the tin under the porch, clean and ready for another day of music. They did not dent it with kicks or drag it down the street. They did not want a bigger noise than the house. Kaila understood that an old pan can be music if there is care. It is only noise if there is hurry. The feathers stayed straight, and the neighbour kept her window open.",
    "At night she told her mum that she had learnt a rhythm and had stopped in time. She had stopped before the yard stopped being pleasant. Her mum kissed her forehead. She did not ask for a show in the kitchen or for a new tin to spoil for fun. The tin stayed where it was, empty and able to sing the next day, if there was calm. If you find an empty tin, do not hit it to spoil it. You can learn a rhythm with someone and stop before your neighbour needs quiet.",
  ],
  "tt:4": [
    "Near San Fernando, at a dirt crossroads, there was a huge kapok tree. Its roots lay like low walls. Kaila arrived in her carnival costume with feathers and a flower in her hair, with her aunt beside her. Her aunt stopped and greeted the trunk with a slow nod of her head. 'Big trees are greeted, not scratched to prove we were here,' she said. Kaila had a smooth stone in her hand, picked up on the way. The idea of a letter in the bark was already half made.",
    "She wanted to draw a letter in the bark, just one line. Then the tree would remember her when other people passed in the afternoon. The stone was good for that, smooth on one side and a little rough on the other. It was perfect for a name. Her aunt looked at her hand and did not take it away by force. She only waited in the shade, without a speech. Kaila looked up at the crown that shaded both roads. The letter felt small and unnecessary. She opened her fingers, and the stone did not touch the trunk. Instead of scratching, she said good morning, very quietly.",
    "She put the stone in her pocket, where it could not mark anything. She took a step back to see the whole tree, from root to crown. The bark stayed old and whole, with no new letters cutting into it or signing it. A costume feather caught on a root, and Kaila freed it carefully without pulling up moss or earth. Her aunt smiled and said the greeting was enough without a signature on the trunk. Two neighbours passed and also nodded their heads, as if it were the most normal thing at that crossroads.",
    "They sat in the shade for a minute. Nobody carved names in the earth with sticks or piled stones against the trunk. Kaila took the stone out of her pocket, looked at it and put it back. Now it was only a smooth keepsake. The kapok tree creaked lightly in the wind, the sound of a big house. Nobody was afraid to stay there. When they moved on, the crossroads looked the same, with the tree in its usual place. Kaila checked that the stone was still in her pocket, far from the bark.",
    "At home, Kaila put the stone on a plate, far from the walls. She told her aunt that the letter had been said aloud, not carved. Her aunt poured fresh water, and the feathers dried at the window. They swayed in the breeze of the San Fernando street. Kaila was glad she had greeted instead of scratching. Her hand no longer reached for the stone to write. If you pass a big tree at a crossroads, keep the stone in your pocket. You can say good morning and leave the bark whole.",
  ],
  "bt:0": [
    "At school in Thimphu, Pema sat in her bright kira. She wore the tego over her shoulders, the koma at her waist and a flower in her hair. The teacher asked for a drawing of the four friends: the bird, the hare, the monkey and the elephant. Together they had planted a seed. Pema pulled the sheet of paper over and covered it with her arm. Then she spoke without looking at her classmate. 'I am doing this drawing on my own,' she said. The classmate stayed on the edge of the bench, pencil still, waiting for room.",
    "The shadow of that tale did not fit into a closed hand. In the tale, each friend did the part it knew. Nobody ended up owning the tree that came later. The elephant brought water with its trunk, and the monkey looked for the sun. The hare cleaned the ground and the bird dropped the seed in the right place. When the tree grew, the shadow was wide enough for all four to sit without pushing. The teacher had told it that morning, slowly, while the room listened in silence. Even so, Pema still wanted all the colours for her own pencil.",
    "Pema drew the elephant's trunk crooked and stopped. She was cross with the paper and with her hurry. She glanced sideways and saw her classmate drawing in the air, without paper. The classmate was drawing the small flight of the bird. It was exactly the part that was missing, and Pema's elbow was still covering it. The flower in her hair moved as she turned, as if it also wanted room. The bench was long enough for two, and the tale had already said so without shouting.",
    "Pema moved her arm away, called her classmate by name and opened the paper between them. 'The bird can be yours, and I will do the elephant,' she said, already smiling a little. Her classmate sat in the centre of the bench without taking the pencil from her hand. She drew the wings calmly. Together they added the hare, the monkey and the tree in the middle, so that the shadow reached all four. Neither signed the drawing alone, and the teacher nodded as she passed without making a speech.",
    "If you want a drawing to be only yours, first remember who might sit beside you. Pema, in Thimphu, called her classmate over. Then the paper found room for the bird, the hare, the monkey and the elephant. The seed in that tale did not belong to one animal, and neither did the shadow. You can love an idea very much and still open your hand. A drawing made by two grows bigger than the fear of sharing.",
  ],
  "bt:1": [
    "In Paro, the rain arrived with a round peal of thunder. Pema ran to the door with the flower still in her hair. She wanted to go out and see the dragon her grandfather talked about. That was the Druk, the dragon of the thunder. Her grandfather held the sleeve of her kira carefully and spoke without raising his voice. 'The Druk does not come to break roofs: he brings rain for the fields,' he said. 'This is the place to listen from.' Pema stopped on the threshold, with one foot almost in the puddle.",
    "Her grandfather put two cups on the low table and poured hot tea with milk, steaming and sweet. It was not a drink to run off with during a thunderstorm. It was there to help you sit and watch the water run straight down the valley window. Pema's tego dried in the warmth of the room. Her hurry went away little by little. Outside, the thunder rolled again. It sounded like a big drum, with no anger in it at all.",
    "Pema held the cup in both hands and did not get up when the sky spoke again. Her grandfather said the fields drink this rain. In the tale, the dragon is a friend of the earth and not a fright. Nobody ran out into the middle of the lightning to prove their courage. The flower in Pema's hair stayed still, well away from the wet wind. The house smelt of tea and warm wood.",
    "When the rain eased, the Paro valley was shining, and the roofs still sang a little. Pema finished the hot tea with milk and only then went to the door, with no thunder above by then. She saw the dark earth, content, and not a trace of damage. She said quietly that the dragon had brought the water. Her grandfather nodded, satisfied. That day the Druk had passed by without anyone stepping into his path.",
    "If you hear thunder and want to run into the rain, first stand at the window with a warm cup. Pema, in Paro, learnt the truth about the tale's dragon. It brings water to the fields and not damage. You can enjoy a big noise and still leave the storm outside. Hot tea with milk waits better than a jump into a puddle. The rain does its work without your running.",
  ],
  "bt:2": [
    "In the Bumthang valley, fine snow had fallen, and the marked trail showed by cloths on the stakes. Pema went with her grandfather, her kira closed tight and the flower tucked in her pocket. High up, her grandfather spoke of the migoi, the shy neighbour of the mountain. The migoi is big and silent and does not like visitors. 'This is not a game: it is someone who prefers snow without footsteps behind him,' her grandfather said. Pema nodded, though curiosity was already in her eyes.",
    "Halfway along, Pema saw deep marks leaving the trail and climbing to one side, where there were no stakes. Her heart jumped, and she wanted to follow those marks and find the snow neighbour. She already had one foot off the beaten ground. Then her grandfather rested his hand on her shoulder, lightly. The snow all around went very quiet, with no invitation in it. The marked trail went on ahead, clear and agreed.",
    "Her grandfather reminded her calmly that you do not follow tracks in the snow. That holds true even when a mark looks like a door. If the migoi was about, it had already seen them. It had already chosen to stay far away. You do not chase a shy neighbour, and you do not call out to make it come off the slope. Pema looked at the marks and imagined a shape turning its face away. She did not imagine a monster waiting. She took a step back, on to the earth by the stakes.",
    "They went back down the trail together, slowly, without shortcuts. Pema did not call out towards the heights. She did not throw snow towards the marks either. She did not ask to go just as far as the next bend. At home she tipped the snow off her boots and told her grandmother what she had not done. There was a quiet pride in her voice. Her grandfather smiled and hung up his coat. The mountain neighbour, shy or only imagined, kept its snow whole.",
    "If you see marks leaving a marked trail in the snow, do not follow them to prove your courage. Pema, in Bumthang, went back to the stakes with her grandfather and left the migoi in peace. That is how you leave a shy neighbour alone. You can be curious and still choose the agreed way. A mountain is not a hiding place to force open. The right way home fits two feet walking side by side.",
  ],
  "bt:3": [
    "At school, Pema drew an animal that looks as if it is made of bits. It has a broad body, short legs and a head that reminds you of other heads. It is called the takin. Her grandfather said it belongs to the mountain even though it looks like no single animal in the books. Her pencil wavered on the outline, and the drawing came out crooked, almost asking for the rubber. She was about to rub it out when she heard a laugh on the bench behind her. The flower in her hair did not protect her from that sudden shame.",
    "A classmate pointed at the sheet and laughed loudly enough for the whole row to hear. The classmate said that was not any animal at all. Pema felt her face go hot and closed her hand over the takin, as if covering a friend. She remembered the valley, where the animal walks without asking to be easy to draw. The teacher looked up, but had not spoken yet. The silence of the room waited to see what Pema would do.",
    "Pema did not answer with another laugh. She said quietly that the drawing was crooked. But the takin of the mountain does not ask for perfect work either. Her classmate shrugged and went back to his own paper, with no more chorus. Later, a girl showed a bird with one wing bigger than the other. The classmate was already opening his mouth. Pema felt the same short, ugly wish to laugh. This time the wish stopped before it came out.",
    "She did not laugh. She lent her rubber and told the girl that the wing could grow in the next stroke. Seeing that nobody was joining in, the classmate went quiet too and lowered his pencil. Pema's takin stayed crooked on the page, and she left it that way. The mountain does not demand a perfect portrait. The flower moved when she nodded to the girl, like a small yes. Both drawings stayed on the table, both accepted.",
    "If you hear a laugh because a drawing is crooked, do not add yours in a hurry. Pema remembered the takin, which looks made of bits. Even so, it belongs to the mountain, and she did not laugh at her classmate's bird. You can see a strange stroke and treat it as a real animal, not as a joke. The pencil had wobbled enough on that page. The room does not need another open mouth.",
  ],
  "bt:4": [
    "On the way near her house, threads between two poles held flags of many colours. They all moved at the same time. Her grandfather said each cloth carries a good wish on the wind. The wind is what carries it, not a child's hand. Pema, in her kira and tego, stopped with the flower in her hair copying the flags. She wanted to untie the yellow one and keep it in her room as if it were hers. The knots, though, had been tied on purpose.",
    "Her fingers came close to the thread, and the thin cloth seemed easy to loosen in one move. If she pulled it off, the wish someone had put there would lose its path. It would lose its colour in the air too. Pema lowered her hand before touching the knot, with her palm empty. It was not a long speech that stopped her. It was the simple idea that the wind had not finished the message yet. The colours went on talking quietly to one another.",
    "Her grandmother had gone to the shop and had not come back yet. The way looked empty up to the bend. Pema looked at the flags and, in a low voice, asked for her grandmother to come home safely. She asked for her with her basket and her usual step. She did not shout the wish for the street to hear. She did not pull a thread to make the wish travel faster. For a moment she stood with quiet hands in front of her koma, watching the yellow rise and fall. The wind carried the message without needing one cloth fewer.",
    "Before long, her grandmother appeared at the bend with the basket at her hip. She gave a wide wave that could be seen from far off. Pema ran to her, not to the flags, and held her free arm on the way back. They passed the threads again, and all the cloths stayed in place, working. Her grandmother offered her a small bread roll from the shop, still warm. The wish had fitted into the wind, whole, and home was closer.",
    "If you see coloured flags tied on a thread, do not pull them down to keep the pretty cloth. Pema left them working and quietly asked for her grandmother to come home safely from the shop. You can have a good wish and trust it to the wind. There is no need to spoil what another person has already hung there. The colours are not a toy for undoing on the path. The homecoming, with the basket and the wave, arrives as the answer.",
  ],
  "kh:0": [
    "In Phnom Penh, Grandmother greeted the neighbour with a calm 'Soursdey'. Then she sat Srey on the step for a story. Srey wore her sampot, a wrap skirt, and the traditional blouse, with a flower in her hair. She listened to the names Vorvong and Sorvong without muddling them. The two brothers had got lost on the road. They called to each other from village to village, never giving each other up. There was no quarrel between them, only the wish to walk side by side again. Srey repeated the names quietly, like someone keeping a key.",
    "In the tale, the brothers met at last beside a wide road. They were tired and happy and meant to go on together. A vain giant, however, sat in the middle. He would not let people pass in peace, because he liked to feel the biggest of all. He did not hit anyone: he simply occupied the way. He waited for the world to admire him. Vorvong spoke with a straight voice, without mockery. Sorvong showed a simple ring, a sign of kindness and not of power. They asked for passage for everyone.",
    "The giant looked at the ring, which shone no brighter than a gesture of respect. He heard that the road was also a home for those who walk. The words did not make him smaller. They recognised his strength and asked him to use it to open, not to block. He stood up, still vain, but no longer barring the way. He let the families go on in peace. Nobody fell and nobody was pushed, and the brothers passed holding hands. The ring stayed on the finger of the one who offered it.",
    "That same day, at the market, a big boy stopped in front of Srey just to show off. He stood with his arms open across the narrow lane. She remembered the giant and did not push him. She did not pull a face either, so that the queue would laugh. She said politely, 'The path belongs to everyone, and you will be fine if you take one step aside.' He blushed, laughed at himself and leaned against the stall. Srey carried on with her grandmother, her flower straight, and the lane moved again.",
    "If you meet someone blocking the way just to seem big, speak clearly and do not shove. Srey, in Phnom Penh, remembered Vorvong and Sorvong. They persuaded the vain giant with words and with a ring of kindness. You can ask for passage for everyone and let peace do the rest of the work. The brothers in the tale walked together again, and the road stayed open. One step aside, asked for in time, works better than a shout.",
  ],
  "kh:1": [
    "At night Grandmother told of the rabbit who lives on the moon. He is there as a memory of sharing. Srey leaned back in her sampot, neatly arranged, to listen. A traveller, tired, sat down at the edge of the scrub. He asked only for a little of whatever there was, without demanding anything of anyone. The rabbit did not put himself in danger and was not hurt. He offered the grass that was his own food, with still paws and a wide heart. The traveller accepted that small offering.",
    "The traveller thanked the rabbit as if it were a feast. Nobody came away wounded from that clearing. In the tale, the moon kept the rabbit's shape. It was so that people would remember sharing whenever they looked up. Srey kept that version: the shape on the moon is a good memory, not a fright. Grandmother pointed at the window. The moon was already round and calm over the roofs. The rabbit up there seemed to be sitting, eating grass in peace, whole and unhurried. Srey smiled and did not ask for a scarier story.",
    "The next day there was a ripe mango at home, sweet and perfumed. Srey wanted to eat the whole of it in the shade of the yard. Grandmother was going to split it calmly with her hands, without a quarrel and without hurry. Srey held the mango against her chest for a moment. Her cousin looked over from a distance, without asking. The flower in her hair brushed her face as she turned towards the table. The sweet smell stayed between the two of them, waiting for a gesture.",
    "Srey split the mango into two and held out the bigger piece to her cousin. She remembered the grass the rabbit offered without being hurt. Her cousin laughed and swapped a piece back so that it was fair. They ate slowly, with the juice on their fingers. Grandmother did not make a speech. She only nodded, like someone watching a tale come down from the moon into the yard. Srey licked a finger and looked at the sky, still light. The mango had been enough for both.",
    "If you have something sweet and see someone waiting, split it and offer part. Do not put yourself in danger to seem big. Srey shared the mango, and the rabbit in the tale had offered his grass without being hurt. The moon keeps his shape as a memory. You can be generous with what fits in your hand and still stay whole. Sharing does not ask for a blow or a hard test. It asks for a piece, a thank you and room in the yard.",
  ],
  "kh:2": [
    "Beside the Mekong river, the water rose gently. Low boats passed without bumping into one another. Srey's mother said that the naga, the river serpent, makes the water rise calmly. That gives the boats a way through, and it is not meant to frighten the banks. Srey, her sampot tucked up and a flower in her hair, stood on the steps. She could see the dark riverbed further out. She wanted to swim out there and see if the serpent would appear. Her mother held her hand before the last wet step.",
    "'A deep place is not an invitation,' her mother said, pointing at the line where the water changes colour. In the tale, the naga works underneath. It lifts the river just enough, and it does not ask for children inside the current. Srey took her foot off the slippery step and stayed on the dry stone, still looking. A boat passed, full of baskets, straight, as if someone below were helping it without a sound. Srey's flower moved in the river wind, not with a jump.",
    "She raised her free hand and waved at the river. It was as if she were greeting the naga without calling it up. She did not go into the water. She did not throw stones to make it answer. She did not laugh at the people rowing. Her mother waved too, more slowly, and the boats kept their calm line. Srey felt that the greeting fitted into the gesture, not into a dive. Her sampot stayed dry, and that was a small, certain victory.",
    "They sat on the top step while the water, now higher, shone unhurried against the bank. Srey counted the boats in a low voice and gave each one a flower's name. She did not go to fetch any of them. When it was time to go, she waved again, from a distance, and the river needed nothing more. Her mother praised the wave, not the courage of someone who throws themselves in. At home Srey drew a gentle serpent under a boat, with low waves and people with dry feet.",
    "If you stand beside a river that is rising, wave from the bank. Do not swim in the deep place to see the tale up close. Srey stayed on the Mekong steps. In the tale the naga makes the river rise calmly for the boats, not for a game. You can love dark water and still leave its work down below. A wave is enough as a greeting. Dry feet on the step are the right ending for that afternoon.",
  ],
  "kh:3": [
    "In the stone courtyard in Siem Reap, the apsara figures stood still on the walls. Each had one knee bent and hands like flowers. Grandmother said those stone dancers teach a slow step. It is done on the ground of the one watching, not on top of the old stones. Srey, in her sampot and blouse, with the flower in her hair, copied the knee in the air. She almost climbed on to a low base to copy it better. Grandmother shook her head gently, with a clear no. The courtyard was big, and the ground was there.",
    "Srey came down before putting her weight on the old stone. She stood barefoot on the smooth earth of the courtyard, where Grandmother had pointed. She copied the step slowly: one foot forward, the knee soft, the hands opening like petals. She was in no hurry to look like a show. The flower in her hair followed the movement and did not fall. One stone apsara seemed to look sideways, busy with its eternal dance. Srey did not touch its carved fingers, nor its necklace, nor its base.",
    "She repeated the step three times, each one slower than the last. In the end her body understood that the beauty was in the calm. Grandmother clapped very quietly, almost a secret. She did not ask her to climb up 'just for a moment' for anyone's photograph. Other people passed and smiled without invading the courtyard with running. Srey finished facing the wall, made a small bow and put her shoes on again. The old stones kept their usual dust, with no new scratch.",
    "On the way home, Srey practised the same step on the earth of the yard, far from the old walls. Her cousin wanted to learn it too. She taught it without telling anyone to climb on anything. Only feet on the ground and hands like flowers. The traditional blouse moved with the gesture. The two laughed when a knee lost its balance, not out of fear. Grandmother brought water and let them repeat until the shade moved. The slow step had fitted into a whole afternoon.",
    "If you see stone dancers, copy the step on the courtyard ground. Do not climb on the old stones to get closer. Srey, in Siem Reap, learnt the slow apsara knee with her feet on the earth, in her sampot and flower. You can admire an ancient figure and leave it whole. Your body can learn slowly at the same time. The dance does not need a jump on to what is old. It needs time, respect and ground that is yours.",
  ],
  "kh:4": [
    "The jasmine rice filled the kitchen with a sweet smell. Srey, still in her sampot, pulled the big bowl closer before Grandmother sat down. She filled her own first, almost to the brim, because the scent seemed to ask for speed and plenty. The flower in her hair leaned over the steam. Grandmother saw, did not scold, and set her empty bowl beside it. She had a smile ready. The rice shone, loose and white, enough for the two of them, with some left for the middle dish.",
    "Grandmother swapped the bowls with a light movement. It was like moving two cups during a game. The full bowl stayed in front of her, and the empty one stayed in front of Srey. Srey opened her mouth and then closed it. There was no sermon and no punishment. There was a smile, and steam rising into the wrong face. Srey looked at the heap of rice in her grandmother's bowl and saw the pattern of the swap. The hand that serves itself first can leave the other without any smell.",
    "Srey laughed at last and asked for the swap back, this time with measure. She filled both bowls equally and still left a little on the dish. It was for whoever came in from the street. Grandmother tasted a spoonful and said the jasmine is better when both bowls steam at once. Srey ate slowly, feeling that the rice did not lose its taste by being shared. The flower straightened when she lifted her face. She was no longer in a hurry to be first.",
    "Later her cousin came in and found the middle dish still warm, with a place made on purpose. Srey did not hide the pan. She did not pretend the rice had run out. She served her cousin the same measure, and the kitchen filled with three equal smells, which were really one. Grandmother winked, remembering the swapping of the bowls. Nobody needed to repeat the lesson aloud. Srey's sampot had a fallen grain on it, and she shook it off, laughing.",
    "If you fill your bowl first, notice who still has an empty bowl beside them. Srey saw the swap her grandmother made with a smile. After that, the jasmine rice had room for both without a speech. You can love the smell and still serve with measure. Leave a little in the middle. What is good does not get smaller by steaming in more than one bowl. The whole kitchen eats better when nobody rushes to hide the pan.",
  ],
  "la:0": [
    "In Luang Prabang, Grandmother greeted the neighbour with a 'Sabaidee'. Then she sat Mali in the shade for an old tale. Mali wore her sinh, a silk skirt, and her silver belt, with a flower in her hair. She heard about a big gourd that came down from the sky. When it was opened, it held no locked treasure. It held enough seeds for every house, and not for just one. Each family took its share and left the rest in the middle, in plain sight. Mali looked at her grandmother's basket.",
    "In the tale, someone tried to hide the best seed inside a sleeve. That house's field grew crooked and alone, with no neighbours laughing beside it. The other seeds were shared, and their plants leaned against one another. Together they made a common shade. Grandmother did not stretch the lesson out. She put three seeds in Mali's palm and said the fat one also came from everyone's basket. Mali closed her fingers slowly, feeling the round grain ask for a pocket. The flower in her hair stayed still, waiting for the choice.",
    "Mali was on her way to her room when she stopped in the corridor. The good seed was still in her hand, and her pocket was open. She remembered the gourd from the sky. It had not chosen one house to be rich and left the others empty. She went back to the kitchen and put the fat seed in the basket again, among the small ones. She did not cover it with a cloth. Grandmother saw and did not clap loudly. She only pushed the basket to the middle of the table. Mali felt her pocket light and her face fresher.",
    "In the morning they planted the seeds in a bed beside the house, in short rows. The fat one went in the middle, not at a hidden edge. Mali watered with a small glass. She did not dig again to peek at who came up first. The neighbour received two equal seeds, and nobody asked which was the best. When the earth went dark, Mali wiped her hands carefully on her sinh, so as not to stain the silk. Her silver belt tapped the bucket lightly, like a tiny bell of work done.",
    "If you find a seed better than the others, do not hide it in your pocket. The sky did not give it only to you. Mali, in Luang Prabang, put the fat seed back in everyone's basket. She remembered the big gourd that brought seeds for all the houses. You can like a good thing and still put it in the middle of the table. The bed grows straighter when no row is secret. The fair share fits in an open hand, not in a closed pocket.",
  ],
  "la:1": [
    "In the market at Vientiane there were brown mushrooms shaped like ears. They were piled in a damp basket, and Mali stopped to laugh at the shape. Grandmother said that, in a tale, the mushrooms kept that shape because someone finally learnt to listen. The earth kept the lesson in the shape of an ear. Mali, in her sinh and silver belt, was already opening her mouth. She wanted to tell what she had seen in the street. Grandmother had started her next sentence, and Mali left it half said. The flower moved when Mali tugged at her sleeve.",
    "Mali interrupted, talking about a dog, a bicycle and a cake all at once. The mushroom tale hung unfinished. Grandmother did not scold. She closed her mouth with a small smile and waited for the hurry to pass. Mali heard her own speech end and saw the silence she had made in her grandmother's sentence. The mushrooms stayed in the basket, quiet, like ears waiting for a whole story. Her shame came softly, with nobody shouting.",
    "'Tell it again, please, because I am listening now,' Mali asked, her hands already still on her knee. Grandmother started again from the beginning, slowly. This time the sentence reached its end with no cut. The tale said that listening is also a harvest. Whoever quiets their hurry finds the rest of the story. And the mushrooms kept their ears so that nobody would forget. Mali nodded in the right places, without jumping ahead. She bought a small handful, not the whole basket, for the evening broth.",
    "At home, while the water heated, Mali let her grandmother finish every sentence before asking a question. The broth smelt of the ear mushrooms, and the kitchen felt like a place that knew how to listen. Mali told her street news only at the end, when Grandmother asked. The news fitted better that way, with no trampling. They ate slowly, the hot soup in deep cups. The flower, a little wilted by then, went into a glass of water, also waiting.",
    "If you interrupt someone who is talking, ask to hear it again and stay quiet until the last word. Mali, in Vientiane, cut off the tale of the ear mushrooms. Then she knew how to go back politely to the start. You can have news burning in your mouth and still let other people's sentences reach the end. Listening is a way of not losing the best of the broth. The ears in the tale are not decoration: they remind everyone of whose turn it is.",
  ],
  "la:2": [
    "At night, on the step above the Mekong, round lights appeared, floating far out. Mali's mother held her by her silver waistband before any idea took hold. People said they were the lights of the naga, seen from the bank. They were beautiful precisely because nobody went to fetch them. Mali, in her sinh and a flower in her hair, felt the dark water call like a game. She wanted to go down the wet steps and into the river to catch a light in her hands. Her mother did not shout.",
    "She only held on firmly and pointed to where the feet should stay. 'The lights are seen from here, and the river at night is not a basket,' her mother said. She kept her voice low so as not to spoil the sight. Mali stayed on the step, barefoot but dry. The lights went on their way without coming closer to be grabbed. One looked like a forgotten lantern, another like a calm eye. None of them asked for company in the water. Her mother sat beside her, shoulder to shoulder.",
    "Her silver belt made a small tinkle as they settled. The sky and the river talked without the help of a dive. Mali pointed, counted and gave names to the lights, always with her body on the step. Her free hand stayed in the air, not in the current. She did not throw a stone to make them jump. She did not ask her mother to go in 'just up to the ankles'. A barge passed far off, with a real lamp. That was another thing, from people awake and on a marked route. The naga lights kept their ownerless shine.",
    "Mali sighed, not with crossness. It was the beauty of something you cannot put in a pocket. When the lights thinned out, her mother stood first and held out her hand. Mali climbed the steps without looking back with envy. At home she lit a small lamp and said that one could be near. It had a place and care. She drew the river on paper, with yellow dots only along the edge of the drawing. She put two figures sitting on the step. The flower went on the table beside it.",
    "If you see pretty lights on a river at night, stay on the step. Stay with the person who is with you. Do not go into the water to catch them. Mali, on the bank of the Mekong, saw the naga lights from the edge and let them go on. She held her mother's hand. You can love something that shines and still not turn it into a dive. Some beauties are only right when your feet are dry. The step is a whole place, not a waiting room for the current.",
  ],
  "la:3": [
    "Mali's uncle filled a bamboo tube with sticky rice and coconut. He sealed it with a leaf and put it by the heat. He had the patience of someone who knows the timing. Mali, in her sinh and silver belt, smelt the sweetness almost at once. She wanted to open the bamboo to see if it was ready. The flower in her hair leaned over the pale smoke. Her uncle shook his head and showed his fingers. There was still a good while to go, and the rice inside was still grain.",
    "It was not yet cake. Opening it early would spoil everyone's work. Mali sat on the bench with her hands under her legs, so as not to reach for the bamboo. She asked questions to make the wait pass. Her uncle said the rice in the bamboo teaches the same thing as a seed. The inside changes when nobody opens it in a hurry to boss it about. Mali looked at the leaf covering the mouth of the tube. She imagined the white going soft, sticky and perfumed. She did not ask for 'just a tiny' test.",
    "The flower slipped to her ear, and she laughed without getting up. When her uncle finally rolled the bamboo away from the heat, the smell filled the yard. Mali almost clapped her hands on the tube. He waited a moment longer, until he could hold it without burning his hands. Only then did he open the leaf. The rice was soft, coming away in short slices, with no raw centre showing. Mali got the first piece on a clean leaf and blew on it, instead of biting it hot. Her silver belt shone as she leaned over to say thank you.",
    "They ate slowly, and Mali said aloud that the wait had been part of the taste, not a mean delay. Her uncle wrapped a piece for Grandmother and another for the neighbour who had lent the coconut. Mali did not eat the pieces meant for the others 'while they are away'. She carried the parcel to her grandmother with both hands. It is how you carry something that has already had its time. The sinh swirled in the corridor, light, and the whole house smelt of coconut.",
    "If you smell something good and want to open the bamboo early, sit down. Wait for the agreed time. Mali learnt that the sticky rice in the bamboo only turns soft when nobody hurries it. Her first piece tasted of patience. You can want something and still let the heat finish the work. What is opened early stays raw in the middle, even with a promising smell. Waiting, here, is a way of cooking.",
  ],
  "la:4": [
    "In the fields outside Vientiane, her uncle's buffalo moved slowly, ploughing a narrow strip. The sun made Mali's silver belt shine. She wore her sinh tucked up so the mud would not eat it. A small flower sat in her hair, already half crooked. The buffalo was not an engine. It was a companion who shared the field with the family, step by step, without being loaded too heavily. Mali took the rope and pulled hard. She wanted the next strip that very morning. The animal stopped, patient and heavy, and did not move on.",
    "Her uncle put his hand over hers and loosened the pull. He made the rope a guide and not a jerk. 'He ploughs if we walk at his pace, not if we drag him,' he said. He showed her the short walk, beside the buffalo's head. Mali copied it, ashamed and watchful. The animal started off again with a calm snort. The earth opened in a dark line, straight enough, with no hurry for a show. Nobody hit it, and nobody shouted a name for the field to hear the anger.",
    "They gave the buffalo water in the shade, in a clean bucket, before starting the second strip. Mali saw that rest was also part of the ploughing. She did not climb on its back to 'go faster'. She did not put more weight on it than the halter. Her uncle spoke quietly, and the buffalo answered with its step. That was its language. Mali wiped the mud from her hands and took the rope again with two fingers, lightly. The flower fell off for good, and she left it at the edge of the field.",
    "When the strip was finished, her uncle loosened the rope a little more. He took the buffalo to the grass, and not round once more 'just because the sun is still up'. Mali walked beside them without pulling any more. She counted her steps to learn the rhythm. At home she told her grandmother that the field is shared with whoever ploughs it, person and animal. A rope hurts if it is a closed arm. Her grandmother poured fresh water for them both. Outside, the buffalo chewed slowly.",
    "If you hold a buffalo's rope, do not pull hard to make the field go at your speed. Mali learnt the slow pace with her uncle, and the animal ploughed without being overloaded. It shared the work the way shade is shared. You can want to finish early and still walk beside it with a light hand. The field opens better when nobody turns a companion into a push. The straight strip is the one made without a jerk.",
  ],
  "mm:0": [
    "In Yangon, Grandmother greeted the neighbour with a 'Mingalaba'. Then she told Hla about the farmer who planted one extra row for the birds. Hla wore her longyi, a wrap-around skirt, and the traditional blouse, with a flower in her hair. She heard that the row was not needed by the house: the rest of the field was enough. The birds ate what was theirs and left the other rows in peace. They seemed to understand the arrangement. Hla looked at the bowl of ripe mangoes on the table.",
    "She was already thinking of the sweet dish. Grandmother had not yet said what to do with the fruit. Hla thought of putting the whole bowl on the window, to look as generous as the farmer. She also thought of putting nothing out, so the mango would be only hers. Neither idea was the one in the tale. The farmer did not give the whole field. He gave one row, and the house went on eating. Hla chose one mango and cut a piece with her grandmother's spoon. She carried it to the window on a clean leaf, and the rest of the bowl stayed on the table.",
    "The birds did not come at once, and Hla almost took the piece back. She was impatient with the empty roof. Grandmother said the extra row is not a show. It is a place that you leave, even if the visitor is slow. Hla sat down to draw, and before long a sparrow landed. It ate without a fuss and went away. It did not take the bowl, did not come into the kitchen and did not ask for the second mango. The flower in Hla's hair moved as she laughed quietly.",
    "She was pleased with the right measure. At lunch the family ate the other mangoes, and nobody said the window had stolen the pudding. Hla told the sparrow story without making it bigger. Grandmother nodded, like someone recognising the farmer from the tale in a small gesture. The leaf at the window was empty and clean, ready for another day if there was spare fruit. Hla did not empty the bowl 'to be good'. She kept the lesson in the size of the piece, which fitted a hand and not a basket.",
    "If you want to share with the birds, leave a piece and not the whole row of the house fruit. Hla, in Yangon, put a bit of mango on the window and kept the rest for the table. The farmer in the tale planted one extra row and still had a harvest. You can be generous without emptying the kitchen. The right measure feeds those who pass and those who live there. A happy sparrow and a full bowl fit in the same day.",
  ],
  "mm:1": [
    "Grandmother told of a wise old man who saw two children pulling on the same basket. He did not shout a name and did not choose a winner. He asked just one question, quietly. The quarrel sat down by itself, because each child had to think. Hla, in her longyi and blouse, with the flower in her hair, found the story too small. Such a small story, for such a result. Just then her cousin came running in with a pencil. Hla held hers out at the same moment. 'It is mine,' they both said, with the same force.",
    "The room suddenly felt narrow. There was only one pencil, blue, and it was for the drawing the teacher had set for the next day. Hla pulled one end and her cousin pulled the other. Neither wanted to let go first, for fear of being left with nothing. Grandmother did not bang on the table. She did not raise her voice to frighten the room. She sat down in the middle and asked one question. Like the wise man in the tale, she said, 'Who needs it now?' The two stopped, fingers still on the pencil.",
    "They were made to look at the work and not at winning. The cousin had to copy a sentence before her mother took her to the shop. Hla could draw after lunch, with the whole afternoon free. Saying it aloud was hard, but it was true. And Grandmother's question left no space for an easy shout. Hla let go of her end and said the hour belonged to her cousin. The pencil would come back after. Her cousin said thank you without celebrating.",
    "She copied the sentence and gave the pencil back, still long. Hla's flower, crooked from the argument, was straightened by Grandmother with two fingers. There was no speech about who was the better person. There was no list of rules pinned to the wall. There was a question, an honest answer and a pencil that went back to its place without a broken point. Hla drew in the afternoon, calmly, and her cousin, back by then, sat beside her. She did not claim the pencil again.",
    "Grandmother passed with tea and did not reopen the subject. The room, which had felt narrow, fitted both of them again. If you argue over a single thing, accept a question instead of a shout. Answer with the true hour. Hla and her cousin let go of the pencil when Grandmother asked who needed it at that moment. That was like the wise man who did not pick winners. You can want a thing very much and still see that it is not your turn.",
  ],
  "mm:2": [
    "Grandmother told the story of someone who was asked for a tiger's whisker. It was as if courage were something you cut off and carry in your pocket. In the tale, that person did not leap on the animal. They did not bring anything to cut with. Instead they came closer slowly, every day, until the tiger grew used to their calm presence. The whisker stayed on the tiger's face, whole. Patience was the real message. Hla, in her longyi, listened with wide eyes and a still flower. In the yard, a wary cat slept far from hands.",
    "Hla wanted to prove the story at once. She reached out to the cat, which bristled and went under the bench with its ears low. She almost pulled its fur to force it to be tame. Her hand stopped in the air, remembering the whisker nobody cut. She sat on the step, sideways, without looking the cat in the eye. She put a bowl of water at a fair distance. She made no high sounds, did not tap on the bench and did not call her cousin in to laugh. The cat stayed where it was, and that was a step too.",
    "The next day Hla went back to the same step, at the same hour, with the same bowl. The cat no longer fled to the back of the yard. On the third day it came to drink while she read, still not letting itself be touched. Hla did not push her finger forward 'to take advantage'. Grandmother watched from the window. She did not say you had to grab the cat for the story to be complete. The tiger's whisker in the tale had taught exactly that.",
    "You come close without taking anything. Hla's flower slipped down over her face, and she did not use it as bait. When the cat at last rested its head against her knee, Hla did not close her hand on the fur. She let the moment end by itself and filled the bowl again. She was happy without a noisy victory. She told her grandmother that the whisker had stayed on the tiger and the fur on the cat. Patience had changed house.",
    "Grandmother laughed quietly and said the yard now had two calm animals. She was counting the girl. Hla thought the sum was fair and went back to the step, no longer in a hurry. If you want a wary animal to trust you, come close slowly. Do not pull its fur to speed up the friendship. Hla practised in the yard what the tiger's whisker tale taught: nobody cuts anything, and patience is the message. You can want to grab and still leave the bowl on the floor. Let your hand stay still. Trust comes when it is not torn away. The cat, the tiger and the afternoon stay whole.",
  ],
  "mm:3": [
    "In Bagan, the bells of a pagoda rang when the wind passed. Each rang at its own time. Hla stood still to listen, and her longyi moved too. The traditional blouse was light, and the flower in her hair followed the sound without ruling it. A cousin showed her the rope of a low bell. He said that if you pulled it, the music came at once, with no waiting for the wind. Hla felt her hand wanting the rope, because the silence between two bells seemed long. Grandmother beside her did not make a speech.",
    "She only looked at the sky, where the real push came from. Hla did touch the rope with two fingers. Then she let go before the pull, like someone closing a door that is not theirs. The bells were not a toy with a time set by children. They were the voice of the wind when it wanted to speak. She sat on a cool step with her hands on her skirt and waited. A breeze came from the plain, and a small bell answered, then another. Nobody had given an order.",
    "Hla smiled at her cousin, who had also kept his hands still, a little ashamed and glad. They listened long enough to learn the difference between a sound forced and a sound that arrives. Hla did not run from bell to bell trying ropes. She did not ask the guard of the place to ring one 'just once' for her. When the wind stopped, the silence was part of the tale too, and nobody filled it with loud laughter. Hla's flower had fine dust from the plain on it.",
    "She shook it off outside the enclosure, already in the shade of the exit. The pagoda kept its bells whole, waiting for the next wish of the air. On the way, her cousin admitted that the pull would have been short and the wind was the better composer. Hla agreed and repeated it at home, for her mother, that she had listened without commanding. Her mother poured fresh water and did not turn the afternoon into a rule pinned to the wall. What had happened was enough.",
    "Quiet hands, a bell that rang by itself, a smile between two cousins. That night Hla lay down still hearing, in her memory, the clear metal over the plain. If you see a bell tied to a rope, do not pull it for fun. You do not pull it just because you want the music now. Hla, in Bagan, waited for the wind, and the pagoda bells rang when they wanted to. You can love a sound and still let its hour arrive from outside. Some music is spoilt if it is torn out.",
  ],
  "mm:4": [
    "On Lake Inle, her uncle steered the boat with an oar tied to one leg. His balance looked easy and was not. Hla, in her longyi and blouse, with the flower pinned against the wind, wanted to stand up straight away. She wanted to see the houses on the water as if from high up. The boat was narrow, and the lake surface pretended to be a floor. Her uncle shook his head, without harshness, and pointed at the seat. 'Sit until I say,' he asked.",
    "And the sentence worked like the oar: one thing at a time. Hla sat in the middle, where the rocking is less. She watched the floating gardens pass at eye level, which was already a whole world. Her uncle rowed with his whole body in agreement. The boat did not need a standing figure to be pretty. She held the edge with one hand. She did not try to stand up 'just for a second' when he looked away. The flower stayed in her hair. The water made a soft sound.",
    "It was the sound of something you respect with your weight well placed. They passed stilt houses and a boat market, and Hla waved with her hand, always sitting. That made her laugh at herself. She wanted the height, but she wanted more not to turn the day into a fright. Her uncle praised her balance, which at that moment was keeping still. He promised a word when the lake was wider and the boat steadier. Hla believed him, because the promise was not a delay for ever.",
    "It was an hour, and hours on the lake are seen in the water. Further on, where there were no other bows nearby, her uncle said her name. He let her come up on to her knees, still not standing, with her hand on his shoulder. It was enough to see further, and the boat stayed straight, with no shout. Hla went back to the seat on her own, happy, and did not ask for the next test. At the jetty she thanked her uncle and the boat, with a light touch on the wood.",
    "Her longyi was dry, the flower in place, and the lake behind them went on working calmly. If you go in a narrow boat, stay seated until the person rowing says you may change position. Hla, on Lake Inle, wanted to stand too early and waited. Her uncle's balance was enough for both of them. You can be curious to see from high up and still leave your weight in the right place. The lake is not a stage for a hurried figure. The trip is beautiful when the boat does not even notice your wish to stand up.",
  ],
};
export default PART;
